<sn><md>### Brief History

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/MID%20Preparation/assets/eveolution-of-soil-fertilizer.jpeg)

- `Manures` and other materials have been applied to soils **for thousands of years** to increase plant growth.
- About **150 years ago**, some `essential plant nutrients` were discovered.
- Subsequently, `fertilizers containing essential plant nutrients` were manufactured and applied to soils to increase fertility.
- By **1855**, researchers at the `Rothamsted Agricultural Experiment Station`, near London, England, declared that `soil fertility could be maintained` permanently with mineral fertilizers.

</md></sn><sn><md>:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/MID%20Preparation/assets/fertilizer-grade.jpeg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
### Fertilizer Grade

- An expression that indicates the `guaranteed minimum percentage of plant nutrients` in a mixed fertilizer containing two or three of the fertilizer elements.
- Order: N, P (expressed as P₂O₅), and K (expressed as K₂O) → **[N–P₂O₅–K₂O]**
- Example: Grade **27-3-9** contains:
  - 27% nitrogen
  - Phosphorus equivalent to 3% P₂O₅
  - Potassium equivalent to 9% K₂O
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Fertilizer Ratio
- The `relative proportions of primary nutrients` in a fertilizer grade `divided by the highest denominator` for that grade.
- Example: A **27-3-9** fertilizer has a ratio of **9-1-3**.
  - High in nitrogen
  - Low in phosphorus
  - Moderate in potassium (relatively speaking)

</md></sn><sn><md>### Fertilizer Materials

- Fertilizers that contain `only one compound` and supply one or two plant nutrients are called **fertilizer materials** or **carriers**.
- The first fertilizer material was manufactured when `ground bones were treated with sulfuric acid` to increase the solubility (availability) of phosphorus.
  - Occurred in England around **1830**
  - Marked the beginning of the modern fertilizer industry
- This was followed by:
  - Development of **potassium materials** from dried sea deposits (late 19th century)
  - Manufacture of **nitrogen materials** from the `fixation of atmospheric N₂` (early 20th century)
</md></sn><sn><md>### Filler
- A substance added to fertilizer materials to:
  - Provide `bulk`
  - Prevent `caking`
  - Serve `some purpose` other than providing essential plant elements
- It makes up the difference between the weight of an added ingredient required and the total fertilizer that should be made according to the fertilizer formula.

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
Common filler materials:
  - Sand
  - Dolomitic limestone
  - Gypsum
  - Furfural
  - Vermiculite
  - Rock salt
  - Coal ashes
  - Chalk grit
  - Spent foundry sand
  - Peanut hulls
  - Saw dust
  - Rice husk
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/MID%20Preparation/assets/common-filler-materials.jpeg)
:::
:::
